1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,519
<font color="#FFFFFF">Social navigation robots have to move safely
through crowds,</font>

2
00:00:04,599 --> 00:00:09,780
<font color="#FFFFFF">and that means perceiving and predicting the paths
of the people around them.</font>

3
00:00:09,860 --> 00:00:12,377
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper introduces the SiT dataset,</font>

4
00:00:12,457 --> 00:00:15,998
<font color="#FFFFFF">short for Socially Interactive Pedestrian Trajectory.</font>

5
00:00:16,078 --> 00:00:23,513
<font color="#FFFFFF">Unlike earlier trajectory datasets captured from
fixed rooftop cameras or from cars driving on separate roads,</font>

6
00:00:23,593 --> 00:00:30,892
<font color="#FFFFFF">SiT was recorded by a mobile robot navigating
densely populated indoor and outdoor spaces in downtown Seoul,</font>

7
00:00:30,972 --> 00:00:34,377
<font color="#FFFFFF">capturing genuine human-robot interaction up close.</font>

8
00:00:34,457 --> 00:00:39,980
<font color="#FFFFFF">It ships synchronized LiDAR, camera, IMU, and RTK
data with 2D and 3D annotations,</font>

9
00:00:40,060 --> 00:00:44,626
<font color="#FFFFFF">semantic maps, and a full benchmark spanning 3D
detection, tracking,</font>

10
00:00:44,706 --> 00:00:48,520
<font color="#FFFFFF">trajectory prediction, and end-to-end motion forecasting.</font>

11
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:51,703
<font color="#FFFFFF">For a robot to navigate safely among people,</font>

12
00:00:51,783 --> 00:00:57,534
<font color="#FFFFFF">it needs to perceive nearby pedestrians as
individual entities in three-dimensional space</font>

13
00:00:57,614 --> 00:01:00,024
<font color="#FFFFFF">and predict where they will move next.</font>

14
00:01:00,104 --> 00:01:06,642
<font color="#FFFFFF">The problem is that the datasets used to train
these models don't reflect the robot's real situation.</font>

15
00:01:06,722 --> 00:01:09,394
<font color="#FFFFFF">Popular pedestrian datasets like ETH, UCY,</font>

16
00:01:09,474 --> 00:01:14,242
<font color="#FFFFFF">and SDD were captured by fixed cameras mounted high
on rooftops or drones,</font>

17
00:01:14,322 --> 00:01:16,994
<font color="#FFFFFF">so they never see human-robot interaction.</font>

18
00:01:17,074 --> 00:01:21,056
<font color="#FFFFFF">Large autonomous-driving datasets do provide rich
sensor data,</font>

19
00:01:21,136 --> 00:01:28,264
<font color="#FFFFFF">but there the vehicle and the pedestrians usually
travel on separate roads and rarely come into close contact.</font>

20
00:01:28,344 --> 00:01:33,440
<font color="#FFFFFF">Neither setting matches a robot weaving through a
crowded hallway or crosswalk.</font>

21
00:01:33,820 --> 00:01:39,736
<font color="#FFFFFF">Studies of human-robot interaction show that a
robot's motion changes how nearby people walk,</font>

22
00:01:39,816 --> 00:01:46,312
<font color="#FFFFFF">and these effects are strongest when the robot and
pedestrians share the same space at close distance.</font>

23
00:01:46,392 --> 00:01:47,859
<font color="#FFFFFF">To study and model that,</font>

24
00:01:47,939 --> 00:01:52,179
<font color="#FFFFFF">we need data collected while a robot actually moves
through crowds,</font>

25
00:01:52,259 --> 00:01:54,694
<font color="#FFFFFF">not from a camera bolted to a building.</font>

26
00:01:54,774 --> 00:02:01,076
<font color="#FFFFFF">Earlier robot datasets came close but each had a
gap: STCrowd kept its sensors at a fixed position,</font>

27
00:02:01,156 --> 00:02:02,624
<font color="#FFFFFF">so scenes barely varied,</font>

28
00:02:02,704 --> 00:02:09,845
<font color="#FFFFFF">and JRDB did not organize its data into
trajectories and did not fully synchronize its multiple sensors in time,</font>

29
00:02:09,925 --> 00:02:11,585
<font color="#FFFFFF">which limits sensor fusion.</font>

30
00:02:11,665 --> 00:02:14,680
<font color="#FFFFFF">SiT is designed specifically to fill these gaps.</font>

31
00:02:15,060 --> 00:02:17,904
<font color="#FFFFFF">The SiT dataset makes several contributions.</font>

32
00:02:17,984 --> 00:02:23,751
<font color="#FFFFFF">It provides large-scale real-world pedestrian
trajectories gathered as a robot navigated</font>

33
00:02:23,831 --> 00:02:27,073
<font color="#FFFFFF">densely populated indoor and outdoor environments,</font>

34
00:02:27,153 --> 00:02:31,791
<font color="#FFFFFF">from building interiors and campuses to crosswalks
and public walkways.</font>

35
00:02:31,871 --> 00:02:35,977
<font color="#FFFFFF">It lets researchers build prediction models using
rich context,</font>

36
00:02:36,057 --> 00:02:41,093
<font color="#FFFFFF">including appearance features, the robot's
ego-motion, and semantic map data.</font>

37
00:02:41,173 --> 00:02:48,336
<font color="#FFFFFF">It achieves precise time synchronization between
all sensor modalities using a centralized triggering method,</font>

38
00:02:48,416 --> 00:02:50,728
<font color="#FFFFFF">which makes sensor fusion practical.</font>

39
00:02:50,808 --> 00:02:55,711
<font color="#FFFFFF">It supplies multi-layered semantic maps for both
indoor and outdoor scenes.</font>

40
00:02:55,791 --> 00:03:01,159
<font color="#FFFFFF">And it offers a curated benchmark covering 3D
detection, 3D multi-object tracking,</font>

41
00:03:01,239 --> 00:03:06,741
<font color="#FFFFFF">trajectory prediction, and an end-to-end task from
perception to motion forecasting,</font>

42
00:03:06,821 --> 00:03:09,000
<font color="#FFFFFF">with everything released publicly.</font>

43
00:03:09,380 --> 00:03:13,946
<font color="#FFFFFF">The data was captured with a Clearpath Husky
unmanned ground vehicle,</font>

44
00:03:14,026 --> 00:03:16,707
<font color="#FFFFFF">remotely operated through downtown Seoul.</font>

45
00:03:16,787 --> 00:03:20,342
<font color="#FFFFFF">The robot carried two sixteen-channel Velodyne LiDARs,</font>

46
00:03:20,422 --> 00:03:24,180
<font color="#FFFFFF">five Basler cameras arranged to cover a full 360 degrees,</font>

47
00:03:24,260 --> 00:03:28,759
<font color="#FFFFFF">two inertial measurement units, and real-time
kinematic positioning.</font>

48
00:03:28,839 --> 00:03:31,048
<font color="#FFFFFF">To fuse camera and LiDAR reliably,</font>

49
00:03:31,128 --> 00:03:38,118
<font color="#FFFFFF">a pulse-per-second signal generator triggers all
the sensors so their data is accurately aligned in time.</font>

50
00:03:38,198 --> 00:03:44,919
<font color="#FFFFFF">The robot's own pose is recovered from RTK outdoors
and from a LiDAR-inertial SLAM algorithm indoors,</font>

51
00:03:44,999 --> 00:03:51,180
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is needed to compensate for the robot's
ego-motion when forming pedestrian trajectories</font>

52
00:03:51,260 --> 00:03:53,268
<font color="#FFFFFF">and to align the semantic maps.</font>

53
00:03:53,348 --> 00:03:59,934
<font color="#FFFFFF">Those maps use a twelve-layer hierarchical
structure built from point clouds and segmented by hand.</font>

54
00:04:00,014 --> 00:04:03,368
<font color="#FFFFFF">Expert annotators labeled 3D cuboids at five hertz,</font>

55
00:04:03,448 --> 00:04:06,061
<font color="#FFFFFF">which were interpolated up to ten hertz,</font>

56
00:04:06,141 --> 00:04:10,774
<font color="#FFFFFF">and 2D boxes were generated from the 3D cuboids
that share object IDs.</font>

57
00:04:10,854 --> 00:04:14,410
<font color="#FFFFFF">For privacy, all faces and license plates are blurred.</font>

58
00:04:14,790 --> 00:04:16,839
<font color="#FFFFFF">In total, SiT contains 60 scenes,</font>

59
00:04:16,919 --> 00:04:22,260
<font color="#FFFFFF">which amounts to about sixty thousand images and
twelve thousand point cloud frames,</font>

60
00:04:22,340 --> 00:04:27,938
<font color="#FFFFFF">carrying roughly four hundred and seventy thousand
two-dimensional annotations and three</font>

61
00:04:28,018 --> 00:04:31,681
<font color="#FFFFFF">hundred and twenty thousand three-dimensional annotations.</font>

62
00:04:31,761 --> 00:04:36,069
<font color="#FFFFFF">Each clip is twenty seconds of sequential data
sampled at ten hertz,</font>

63
00:04:36,149 --> 00:04:40,263
<font color="#FFFFFF">producing nine seconds of trajectory represented as
pose vectors.</font>

64
00:04:40,343 --> 00:04:43,812
<font color="#FFFFFF">On top of this data, the paper defines four benchmarks.</font>

65
00:04:43,892 --> 00:04:51,168
<font color="#FFFFFF">Three-D pedestrian detection is scored with average
precision based on center distance at thresholds of a quarter,</font>

66
00:04:51,248 --> 00:04:52,975
<font color="#FFFFFF">a half, one, and two meters.</font>

67
00:04:53,055 --> 00:04:56,911
<font color="#FFFFFF">Three-D tracking uses standard multi-object
tracking metrics.</font>

68
00:04:56,991 --> 00:05:02,590
<font color="#FFFFFF">Trajectory prediction takes two seconds of past
motion and predicts seven seconds ahead,</font>

69
00:05:02,670 --> 00:05:08,462
<font color="#FFFFFF">scored with average and final displacement error
over the best of K candidate trajectories.</font>

70
00:05:08,542 --> 00:05:14,850
<font color="#FFFFFF">And an end-to-end task runs from raw sensors all
the way to future bounding boxes and trajectories.</font>

71
00:05:15,230 --> 00:05:20,637
<font color="#FFFFFF">The data analysis confirms that SiT captures
exactly the interactions it set out to.</font>

72
00:05:20,717 --> 00:05:26,124
<font color="#FFFFFF">When you plot where pedestrians appear relative to
the robot, they cluster close in,</font>

73
00:05:26,204 --> 00:05:27,431
<font color="#FFFFFF">from all directions,</font>

74
00:05:27,511 --> 00:05:33,506
<font color="#FFFFFF">whereas in Waymo Open and nuScenes pedestrians tend
to sit off to the side on separate paths.</font>

75
00:05:33,586 --> 00:05:40,430
<font color="#FFFFFF">Counting instances that satisfy both a
space-sharing condition and a within-two-meter proximity condition,</font>

76
00:05:40,510 --> 00:05:44,938
<font color="#FFFFFF">SiT has dramatically more than the three
autonomous-driving datasets,</font>

77
00:05:45,018 --> 00:05:52,189
<font color="#FFFFFF">which means driving data underrepresents the
human-robot interaction that social navigation robots must handle.</font>

78
00:05:52,269 --> 00:05:53,756
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the benchmark itself,</font>

79
00:05:53,836 --> 00:06:00,485
<font color="#FFFFFF">the standout finding is that supplying the semantic
map improves trajectory prediction: the best model,</font>

80
00:06:00,565 --> 00:06:01,856
<font color="#FFFFFF">NSP-SFM with the map,</font>

81
00:06:01,936 --> 00:06:06,298
<font color="#FFFFFF">reaches an ADE-twenty of about 0.52 and an
FDE-twenty of about 0.93,</font>

82
00:06:06,378 --> 00:06:09,630
<font color="#FFFFFF">clearly better than the same model without the map.</font>

83
00:06:10,010 --> 00:06:14,535
<font color="#FFFFFF">The most informative comparison in the paper is
turning the semantic map on and off for trajectory prediction.</font>

84
00:06:14,615 --> 00:06:19,141
<font color="#FFFFFF">For Y-Net,</font>

85
00:06:19,221 --> 00:06:23,746
<font color="#FFFFFF">adding the map lowers the ADE-twenty from about
0.84 down to 0.68 and the FDE-twenty from about 1.88 to 1.55.</font>

86
00:06:23,826 --> 00:06:28,352
<font color="#FFFFFF">For NSP-SFM,</font>

87
00:06:28,432 --> 00:06:32,957
<font color="#FFFFFF">the map brings ADE-twenty from about 0.63 to 0.52
and FDE-twenty from about 1.09 to 0.93.</font>

88
00:06:33,037 --> 00:06:37,563
<font color="#FFFFFF">In both cases the map helps,</font>

89
00:06:37,643 --> 00:06:42,168
<font color="#FFFFFF">confirming the value of the scene context that SiT
provides.</font>

90
00:06:42,248 --> 00:06:46,774
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the detection side,</font>

91
00:06:46,854 --> 00:06:51,379
<font color="#FFFFFF">models that fuse camera and LiDAR outperform
single-sensor models,</font>

92
00:06:51,459 --> 00:06:55,985
<font color="#FFFFFF">and voxel-based backbones outperform pillar-based ones,</font>

93
00:06:56,065 --> 00:07:00,590
<font color="#FFFFFF">with TransFusion using a voxel backbone giving the
best detection score.</font>

94
00:07:00,970 --> 00:07:04,960
<font color="#FFFFFF">Here are the numbers worth remembering.</font>

95
00:07:05,040 --> 00:07:09,030
<font color="#FFFFFF">SiT has sixty scenes,</font>

96
00:07:09,110 --> 00:07:13,100
<font color="#FFFFFF">about sixty thousand images and twelve thousand
point cloud frames,</font>

97
00:07:13,180 --> 00:07:17,170
<font color="#FFFFFF">with roughly four hundred and seventy thousand
two-dimensional and three hundred and twenty</font>

98
00:07:17,250 --> 00:07:21,240
<font color="#FFFFFF">thousand three-dimensional annotations.</font>

99
00:07:21,320 --> 00:07:25,310
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the trajectory benchmark,</font>

100
00:07:25,390 --> 00:07:29,380
<font color="#FFFFFF">the best model with the semantic map reaches an
ADE-twenty of about 0.52 and an FDE-twenty of about 0.93.</font>

101
00:07:29,460 --> 00:07:33,450
<font color="#FFFFFF">On detection,</font>

102
00:07:33,530 --> 00:07:37,520
<font color="#FFFFFF">the best camera-plus-LiDAR fusion model reaches a
mean average precision of about 0.53.</font>

103
00:07:37,600 --> 00:07:41,590
<font color="#FFFFFF">And on tracking, the CenterPoint tracker leads with
an sAMOTA around 0.61.</font>

104
00:07:41,970 --> 00:07:43,282
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is simple.</font>

105
00:07:43,362 --> 00:07:47,700
<font color="#FFFFFF">If you want to build robots that move safely and
gracefully among people,</font>

106
00:07:47,780 --> 00:07:51,694
<font color="#FFFFFF">you need training data that shows the robot up
close in the crowd,</font>

107
00:07:51,774 --> 00:07:56,899
<font color="#FFFFFF">with all its sensors aligned in time and with the
surrounding scene captured as a map.</font>

108
00:07:56,979 --> 00:08:00,106
<font color="#FFFFFF">SiT is the first dataset to deliver that combination,</font>

109
00:08:00,186 --> 00:08:04,282
<font color="#FFFFFF">together with a benchmark that spans detection,
tracking, prediction,</font>

110
00:08:04,362 --> 00:08:05,916
<font color="#FFFFFF">and end-to-end forecasting,</font>

111
00:08:05,996 --> 00:08:09,850
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it is all publicly released so the community
can build on it.</font>
